**user**:
> In ggplot, I have both a fill and a pattern, with different legends. But the legend that shows the fill colors shows them with one of the patterns, and I just want it to show them without a pattern overlaid. How do I do that?
**assistant**:
> You can use the `guides()` function along with `override.aes` to customize how the aesthetics appear in your legends without affecting the main plot itself. Here's how you can modify the fill legend to display the colors without any pattern:
> 
> ```r
> library(ggplot2)
> library(ggpattern)  # Only necessary if you are using the pattern functionality
> 
> # Assume ggplot object is called 'p'
> p + 
>   guides(
>     fill = guide_legend(
>       override.aes = list(pattern = "none")  # Use "none" or an equivalent to indicate no pattern
>     )
>   )
> ```
> 
> Make sure to replace `p` with whatever your ggplot object is named. The key part is inside the `override.aes` where you set `pattern = "none"`, or the equivalent in the pattern library you are using. This tells ggplot to draw the fill legend without any patterns, while your actual plot still uses them.
